OWASP Category

OWASP Top 10 2021 – A05: Security Misconfiguration

Description

It was observed that the application is configured with an overly permissive Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) policy. The HTTP response header includes:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

This configuration allows any external domain to access the application resources without restriction. Additionally, multiple HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS) and headers are allowed, increasing the exposure surface.

Although authentication may still be required, such a permissive CORS policy weakens the browser’s same-origin policy and can enable unauthorized cross-origin interactions.

Impact

An attacker can:

Perform cross-origin requests from malicious domains
Potentially access sensitive data if combined with authentication/session weaknesses
Launch phishing or malicious web applications interacting with the target system
Abuse APIs exposed via the application
Increase risk of data exfiltration when combined with other vulnerabilities

The impact becomes significantly higher if sensitive endpoints are exposed or if credentials are improperly handled.

Proof of Concept (PoC Steps)

Step 1: Intercept any application request using Burp Suite

Step 2: Observe the response headers:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

Step 3: Create a malicious HTML page on another domain:

<script>
fetch("https://target-app-url", {
  method: "GET"
})
.then(res => res.text())
.then(data => console.log(data));
</script>

Step 4: Open the malicious page in a browser

Step 5: Verify that the request is successfully processed due to permissive CORS policy

Recommendations

It is recommended to implement a strict and secure CORS policy:

Restrict Access-Control-Allow-Origin to trusted domains only
Avoid using wildcard (*) in production environments
Limit allowed HTTP methods to only required ones
Restrict allowed headers to necessary values
Avoid exposing sensitive endpoints via cross-origin requests
Implement proper authentication and authorization checks
Validate origin headers on the server side
Regularly review and audit CORS configurations
References
https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
https://portswigger.net/web-security/cors
